# ATTENTION-TERM: issue 85 — the term is added, because the measurement refuted the alternative

Issue 85 offered two resolutions and asked for one with a reason. **Resolution 2 is refuted.**
`mx.fast.scaled_dot_product_attention` has no fused vector-Jacobian product: differentiating it
falls back to a formulation that builds `q @ kᵀ`. The evidence is
`research/records/ATTENTION-TERM-2026-09-08` (committed at d17f9d0), measured on synthetic
tensors with no checkpoint loaded.

Two facts came out of it, and they need separate statements.

**The backward materialises the scores at every head dimension** — 64, 128 and 256 give the same
quadratic within a few percent. Nothing about this model causes it, and no head dimension avoids
it.

**The forward materialises them only at head dimension 256, which is this model's.** At 64 and
128 the forward peak equals the input tensors' bytes *exactly*, at every length; at 256 it does
not. The 4B's eight full-attention blocks are off MLX's fused forward path.

## The coefficient

**102.3 bytes per token², one attention block**, fitted on the two largest rows where the linear
terms have washed out. That is 3.20 bare bfloat16 score matrices: the backward keeps the softmax
output its vjp needs and a wider accumulation of the score gradient as well as the scores. The
decomposition is not claimed — the coefficient is measured.

The issue's own arithmetic was wrong in both directions and about 2.6× high overall. It assumed
eight blocks × one score matrix; the truth is one block (gradient checkpointing bounds the live
graph to a single decoder layer, the same reason `retained_recurrence_layers` is 1) × 3.2 copies.
At 8,192 tokens one block's backward measures **6.672 GiB**, against the 17.2 GB the issue
projected.

## What changed in the gate

`_estimated_peak_gib` was affine — `slope × tokens + intercept` — for every mode. It is now that
plus `blocks × 102.3 × tokens² / 2³⁰`. The two parts are kept separate rather than folded into one
fit, because they answer to different evidence: the affine part to `_CALIBRATION_POINTS`, the
quadratic part to the record. Editing either alone stays meaningful.

Every envelope is re-fitted on the **residual** — each measured peak minus the term at its own row
length — so the affine coefficients describe what is left after it instead of absorbing part of
it. Adding the term back at evaluation leaves every calibration point still cleared; what changes
is how the envelope extrapolates past 2,874 tokens.

| mode | slope MiB/token | intercept GiB | row ceiling at 17.76 GiB |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| floor | 2.114 | 3.207 | 5,077 |
| unrolled | 16.859 | 3.207 | 782 |
| chunked | 8.039 | 4.738 | 1,428 |
| chunkwise | 2.114 | 3.399 | 5,013 |

The floor's slope was 2.495 MiB/token. **15.3 percent of it was the attention term absorbed as
though it were linear** — which is why the fit looked good: over 997–2,874 tokens a quadratic is
well approximated by a line, and the line took the term's average rate across that window. Right
in the window, low outside it.

## Stated without inflation

**No decision the gate makes today changes.** The chunkwise ceiling moves from 5,347 to 5,013
tokens, six percent. Arm A trains at a 2,688-token cap, comfortably inside both; 8,192 is refused
either way. The correction matters for the reason the issue gave rather than for a decision it
flips: an efficiency probe reaching for long rows was measuring against a gate whose shape had
never been checked at those lengths. At 32,768 tokens the term alone is 102.3 GiB for one block
against a 17.76 GiB working set, so no 32k training row is feasible on this machine and the
preflight now says so.

## Row ceiling, as the acceptance asked

`row_ceiling_tokens` is in each mode's estimate and on the footprint block for the gating mode.
Solved rather than searched — the envelope is a quadratic — and tested against the gate itself at
both sides of the boundary, so a ceiling that drifts from what `fits_with_headroom` would say
fails.

## `_activation_bytes`, the issue's literal target

Now hidden states plus the score term. The score term is per sequence, so it multiplies the batch
rather than squaring with it; that is a structural statement, not a measurement, and it is said in
the docstring because the probe ran at batch 1.

## The one place this scales for a departure, and why that is not a contradiction

`preflight.py` refuses to scale the envelope for anything but the row length, and names departures
instead. The block count is the exception, and the distinction is worth being precise about: the
term is not a fit to whole-model peaks, it is a per-block cost measured on its own, and how many
blocks are live at the peak is **counted from the architecture** rather than fitted — one under
checkpointing, all of them without it. Multiplying a measured per-block cost by a counted number
of blocks is a different act from scaling an empirical envelope by a variable it never saw.

It is still named in `calibration_domain_departures` whenever the count is not one, so a reader
can see which half was measured. If the Chief reads the rule more strictly, the fix is to pin
`blocks=1` always and let the existing checkpointing departure carry it; that errs low, which is
the wrong direction for a gate, which is why I did not.

## Verification

```
full suite:                     exit 0
tests/test_preflight.py:        exit 0, 4 new tests
ruff, both changed files:       0 findings before, 0 after
git apply --check against HEAD: 0
every calibration point still sits under its envelope with the term added back
the probes: no checkpoint loaded; largest peak 6.67 GiB against a 10.66 GiB R47 cap
concurrency: no libmlx-mapped process and no lock, checked in each launching command
```

Six existing tests changed meaning and are re-pinned deliberately, not relaxed: five
re-derive the reported peak from the artifact and now add the quadratic part, and one
pinned the memory block's byte counts. The re-derivation test asserts the artifact carries
everything needed to rebuild the number, so it gained the new fields rather than losing the
assertion.

